  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Near-normal temperatures this upcoming week, with dry\n  conditions. \n\n- Increasing winds Tuesday, especially across northwest Montana\n  and the higher terrain.  \n\n- Confidence increasing for a warm, dry early October beyond the \n  7 day forecast. \n\nGOES Satellite imagery early this morning shows an upper level \nlow spinning off the southern Oregon/northern California coast, \nwith southwesterly flow aloft bringing a shield of cirrus clouds \nover the Northern Rockies. This low will begin to spread inland \nthrough the afternoon, with widespread cloud cover moving \noverhead. Many locations this morning across western Montana and \nhigher mountain valleys of Idaho are waking up to freezing \ntemperatures. Strong valley inversions have been observed and with\nclouds moving in, temperatures will be slow to warm as inversions\nare slow to break until early-mid afternoon. Scattered showers \nare also forecast across Lemhi County, ID into southwest Montana, \nwhere weak synoptic forcing will provide sufficient lift for \nshower development. Little to any precipitation will accompany \nthese showers. \n\nA westerly flow pattern will take shape across the area Monday\ninto Wednesday. Model guidance portrays a shortwave trough moving\nalong the USA/Canada border Tuesday afternoon through Wednesday\nmorning. Showers will develop along the ID/MT border and along the\ndivide in northwest Montana as this system pushes through.\nOverall, precipitation amounts will be on the lower side, with\nonly a 40% chance for 0.10 inches or more of precipitation. This\nsystem will bring an increase in westerly winds. Peak gusts will \nreach 20-25 mph, locally to 30 mph across western Montana valleys\nand higher elevations of Idaho. The highest gusts will impact \nmountain areas along the divide in Northwest Montana, including \nGlacier Park, as wind gusts reach 30-50 mph. \n\nHigh pressure returns Thursday into Friday, with guidance\nsuggesting a quick hitting shortwave trough moving along the\nUSA/Canada border on Saturday. Ensemble guidance has been trending\nweaker with this trough passage, with only 10% of the membership\nsuggesting widespread precipitation and cooler temperatures with a\ncold frontal passage across the area. The remaining 90% of\nguidance suggests an increase in winds, along with isolated\nshowers across northwest Montana, with mainly dry conditions area\nwide. \n\nConfidence increasing for a warm, dry early October beyond the 7 \nday forecast. The large scale weather pattern is being influenced \nby upstream tropical activity. With 3 hurricanes in the central \nand east Pacific Ocean from Hawaii to Baja and a typhoon \napproaching the eastern periphery of Japan, it's been a difficult \nforecast for models. However, they're starting to coalesce around \nhigher chances for a potent ridge over the northern Rockies into \nthe first third of October. Ensemble mean temperature forecasts \nare coming in 15 degrees above seasonal averages at about 9-12 \ndays out. Those are large anomalies for that long of a lead time! \nAdd in that the signal is coming from multiple ensemble systems \nincluding both dynamical (physics based) and AI modeling systems, \nthis provides above normal levels of confidence for such a long \nlead time forecast. Looking back, the last time this kind of warm \nridge set up over the northwestern US in early October was october\n9-11 and 14, 1991. Eureka, Montana hit 86 degrees, Grangeville, \nIdaho 87 degrees, and Butte 81 degrees! The Center for Climate \nPrediction has issued forecasts for the Northern Rockies region \nwith a 60-70% chance of above average temperatures and \nprecipitation forecasts are leaning below average for that time \nperiod as well.",
